      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Rome’s real weapon was organization."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.029.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Citizens under arms",
      "body": "In the Republic, a legion had roughly 4,000 to 5,000 infantry. In the earlier Republic, legionaries were property-owning citizens who bought their own equipment. Each carried a short stabbing sword, the gladius, and heavy throwing javelins called pila."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.029.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Flexible blocks",
      "body": "Legions fought in maniples, small units that could move and turn independently. They were arranged in three lines: the young hastati in front, the principes behind them, and the veteran triarii at the back.",
      "callout": "Hastati → principes → triarii"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.029.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "The pilum was built to bend.",
      "context": "Its long iron shank was designed to bend on impact, so an enemy couldn’t simply throw it back at the Romans."
    },
    {
